    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166813,
      "id": "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b",
      "title": "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4dc2eb29-d407-5cca-814a-e3e5ea0f0d4c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are eighth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}})^{5}+{x}^{10}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}$\n- **II.** ${x}^{10}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{4}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{5}={x}^{10}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of eighth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ so this is a fourth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}.$\n\n- Equation III is also a eighth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
