    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8a0ff6de-a806-5122-8660-857dd11544dc",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are fifth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}})^{3}+{x}^{7}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{‴}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}})^{2}+{x}^{9}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}})^{4}={x}^{5}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of fifth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ so this is a sixth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}},$ so this is a seventh-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a fifth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ so this is a fourth-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"none.\"",
