      ":question/id": "a3a02a33-c918-5f90-9b66-060931d98193",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are seventh-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{3}+{x}^{10}{y}^{′}=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{4}={x}^{9}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{9}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}})^{4}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of seventh-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ so this is a eighth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ so this is a eighth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ so this is a fourth-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"none.\"",
