  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166813,
      "id": "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b",
      "title": "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "71f44a31-042f-592d-9209-898d8a9c439f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are seventh-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{‴}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}})^{5}={x}^{7}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}$\n- **II.** ${x}^{9}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}={y}^{‴}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}})^{2}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{7}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}})^{3}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of seventh-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ so this is a sixth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}},$ so this is a fifth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}},$ so this is a fifth-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"none.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "1562c67d-d789-5606-8dd1-5f08c6dd4636",
