      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are fourth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}})^{5}={x}^{9}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\n- **II.** ${x}^{10}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}})^{2}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{10}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}})^{5}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of fourth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a fourth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ so this is a sixth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a fourth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}},$ so this is a fifth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a fourth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}},$ so this is a seventh-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"none.\"",
