      ":question/id": "95896d91-42d4-58c9-800d-ef7496379b30",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are sixth-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}})^{5}={x}^{10}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}$\n- **II.** ${x}^{8}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}})^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{5}={x}^{9}{y}^{′}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of sixth-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a sixth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ so this is a fourth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a sixth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}},$ so this is a seventh-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a sixth-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ so this is a eighth-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"none.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c0bd2cc0-1a4d-5da1-aa1b-e767f20a0dac",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
